\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Setup}
\label{sec:problem-setup}

Consider a pretraining corpus $\mathcal{D} = \{x_1, \ldots, x_N\}$ comprising an unknown mixture of human-authored text $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{real}}$ and machine-generated text $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{synth}}$, with contamination rate $\rho = |\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{synth}}| / |\mathcal{D}|$ unknown to the practitioner. A pretrained contamination detector assigns each sample a synthetic probability $q(x_i) \in [0, 1]$.

Following \cite{drayson2025detection}, we define per-sample weights as
\begin{equation}
    w_b(x_i) = (1 - q(x_i))^b, \quad b \geq 0,
    \label{eq:weight}
\end{equation}
where $b = 0$ recovers uniform weighting and increasing $b$ progressively downweights samples with high synthetic probability. The weighted pretraining objective is
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}(b) = \frac{1}{\sum_j w_b(x_j)} \sum_{i} w_b(x_i) \, \ell(x_i; \theta),
    \label{eq:loss}
\end{equation}
where $\ell(x_i; \theta)$ is the per-sample language modeling loss.

\cite{drayson2025detection} treat $b$ as a hyperparameter selected via validation-based grid search, requiring separate training runs for each candidate value. Their optimal $b$ varies across model families (e.g., $b{=}10$ for GPT-2 and SmolLM $\leq$360M vs.\ $b{=}1$ for SmolLM 1.7B), making per-model tuning unavoidable. We instead derive $b$ adaptively per batch from the detector scores alone, requiring no validation set and no grid search.

\subsection{Dual-Constraint Framework}
\label{sec:dual-constraint}

The exponent $b$ controls a fundamental trade-off: stronger downweighting (higher $b$) removes more synthetic influence but also discards effective training signal. We formalize this trade-off through two per-batch statistics.

\paragraph{Effective real proportion.} For a batch $\mathcal{B} = \{x_1, \ldots, x_B\}$ with weights $w_i = w_b(x_i)$, we define
\begin{equation}
    \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b) = \frac{\sum_{i \in \mathcal{B}} w_i(b) \cdot \mathbf{1}[x_i \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{real}}]}{\sum_{i \in \mathcal{B}} w_i(b)},
    \label{eq:alpha_eff}
\end{equation}
measuring the effective fraction of real data in the weighted batch. Since true provenance labels are unavailable at training time, we approximate $\mathbf{1}[x_i \in \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{real}}]$ with $\mathbf{1}[q(x_i) < 0.5]$; all theoretical properties below hold for this proxy as well.

\paragraph{Effective sample size.} We use Kish's effective sample size \cite{vehtari2024psis}:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{ESS}(b) = \frac{\left(\sum_{i \in \mathcal{B}} w_i(b)\right)^2}{\sum_{i \in \mathcal{B}} w_i^2(b)},
    \label{eq:ess}
\end{equation}
which quantifies how many equally weighted samples the weighted batch is worth. $\mathrm{ESS}(0) = B$ (uniform weights) and $\mathrm{ESS}(b) \to 1$ as $b \to \infty$ for any batch containing synthetic samples.

\begin{proposition}[Monotonicity]
\label{prop:monotonicity}
For any batch containing samples with non-identical $q$-scores:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
    \item $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ is strictly increasing in $b$.
    \item $\mathrm{ESS}(b)$ is strictly decreasing in $b$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For (1), differentiating Eq.~\eqref{eq:alpha_eff} with respect to $b$ and using $\frac{dw_i}{db} = w_i \ln(1 - q_i)$ yields
\begin{equation}
    \frac{d\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}}{db} = \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(1 - \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}) \left( \mathbb{E}_w[\ln(1{-}q) \mid \mathrm{real}] - \mathbb{E}_w[\ln(1{-}q) \mid \mathrm{synth}] \right),
    \label{eq:dalpha}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbb{E}_w$ denotes expectation under the normalized weight distribution. Since real samples have lower $q$-scores, $\ln(1{-}q)$ is strictly less negative for real than synthetic samples, making the difference positive. For (2), increasing $b$ concentrates weight mass on fewer low-$q$ samples, strictly reducing ESS when weights are non-uniform. Full proofs are in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}.
\end{proof}

Proposition~\ref{prop:monotonicity} establishes that quality and sample efficiency move in opposite directions as $b$ increases. This motivates two complementary constraints.

\paragraph{Quality lower bound.} We require $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b) \geq \tau$, where $\tau \in (0, 1)$ is a quality threshold. By strict monotonicity of $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$, this defines a unique lower bound
\begin{equation}
    b_\tau = \inf\{b \geq 0 : \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b) \geq \tau\}
    \label{eq:b_tau}
\end{equation}
on the exponent---the minimum intervention needed to achieve quality $\tau$. \cite{wang2026survive} show that convergence rate scales with the effective real data fraction, providing theoretical motivation for maintaining $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$ above a threshold.

\paragraph{Stability upper bound.} We require $\mathrm{ESS}(b) \geq n_{\min}$, where $n_{\min} \geq 1$ is a minimum effective sample size. By strict monotonicity of ESS, this defines a unique upper bound
\begin{equation}
    b_{\mathrm{ESS}} = \sup\{b \geq 0 : \mathrm{ESS}(b) \geq n_{\min}\}
    \label{eq:b_ess}
\end{equation}
on the exponent---beyond which gradient estimates become unreliable due to weight concentration.

\paragraph{Minimum intervention principle.} We select
\begin{equation}
    b^* = \begin{cases}
        0 & \text{if } \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(0) \geq \tau \\
        b_\tau & \text{if } b_\tau \leq b_{\mathrm{ESS}} \\
        b_{\mathrm{ESS}} & \text{if } b_\tau > b_{\mathrm{ESS}}
    \end{cases}
    \label{eq:bstar}
\end{equation}
yielding three regimes: (1)~\textbf{Already clean} ($\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(0) \geq \tau$): the unweighted batch meets the quality threshold; $b^* = 0$. (2)~\textbf{$\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$-binding} ($b_\tau < b_{\mathrm{ESS}}$): the quality constraint determines $b^*$ with ESS margin remaining; the method applies the \emph{minimum} intervention to reach quality $\tau$. (3)~\textbf{ESS-binding} ($b_\tau \geq b_{\mathrm{ESS}}$): the quality threshold is infeasible under the stability constraint; the method applies the maximum stable intervention $b_{\mathrm{ESS}}$.

The distinction between regimes 2 and 3 is central to the minimum intervention principle: when the feasible region $[b_\tau, b_{\mathrm{ESS}}]$ is non-empty, the method selects its left endpoint, downweighting no more than necessary. In our experiments ($\tau = 0.8$, $n_{\min} = 2$, $\rho = 0.4$), 40.1\% of reweighted batches fall in regime~2, where the method selects mean $b^* = 1.67$ compared to $b^* = 4.15$ from an ESS-only approach---a $2.5\times$ less aggressive exponent, retaining $\mathrm{ESS} = 2.60$ well above the floor $n_{\min} = 2$.

\subsection{Algorithm}
\label{sec:algorithm}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:solver} implements per-batch $b^*$ selection via bisection on $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Per-batch adaptive exponent solver}
\label{alg:solver}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Batch scores $\{q_1, \ldots, q_B\}$, threshold $\tau$, ESS floor $n_{\min}$, tolerance $\varepsilon$, cap $b_{\max}$
\Ensure Optimal exponent $b^*$
\State Compute $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(0)$ via Eq.~\eqref{eq:alpha_eff} with $b = 0$
\If{$\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(0) \geq \tau$}
    \State \Return $b^* = 0$ \Comment{Already clean}
\EndIf
\State $b_{\mathrm{lo}} \gets 0, \; b_{\mathrm{hi}} \gets b_{\max}$
\While{$b_{\mathrm{hi}} - b_{\mathrm{lo}} > \varepsilon$} \Comment{Bisect for $b_\tau$}
    \State $b_{\mathrm{mid}} \gets (b_{\mathrm{lo}} + b_{\mathrm{hi}}) / 2$
    \If{$\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b_{\mathrm{mid}}) < \tau$}
        \State $b_{\mathrm{lo}} \gets b_{\mathrm{mid}}$
    \Else
        \State $b_{\mathrm{hi}} \gets b_{\mathrm{mid}}$
    \EndIf
\EndWhile
\State $b_\tau \gets b_{\mathrm{hi}}$
\If{$\mathrm{ESS}(b_\tau) \geq n_{\min}$} \Comment{$\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$-binding}
    \State \Return $b^* = b_\tau$
\Else \Comment{ESS-binding}
    \State Find $b_{\mathrm{ESS}} = \sup\{b : \mathrm{ESS}(b) \geq n_{\min}\}$ via bisection
    \State \Return $b^* = b_{\mathrm{ESS}}$
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\paragraph{Implementation.} We implement reweighting at the loss level: each sample's loss is scaled by its normalized weight $\bar{w}_i = w_{b^*}(x_i) / \sum_j w_{b^*}(x_j)$, yielding continuous gradient contributions from all samples including those with intermediate $q$-scores. This contrasts with the sampling-importance-resampling (SIR) approach of \cite{drayson2025detection}, which discretely resamples a subset of the batch, introducing additional variance and discarding partial information from borderline samples.

\paragraph{Computational cost.} Each evaluation of $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and ESS is $O(B)$, and bisection requires $O(\log(b_{\max} / \varepsilon))$ iterations. The total per-batch overhead is $O(B \log(b_{\max} / \varepsilon))$, negligible compared to the $O(BHD)$ cost of the forward and backward passes through a transformer with hidden dimension $H$ and depth $D$.

\subsection{Hyperparameters}
\label{sec:hyperparameters}

The dual-constraint framework introduces two hyperparameters: the quality threshold $\tau$ and the ESS floor $n_{\min}$.

\paragraph{Quality threshold $\tau$.} \cite{wang2026survive} establish that pretraining convergence rate scales with the effective real data proportion, motivating the use of a quality threshold. However, their analysis assumes known mixture proportions; since we operate without this assumption, $\tau$ is an empirical hyperparameter with theoretical motivation rather than a theoretically derived quantity.

We evaluate $\tau \in \{0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9, 0.95\}$ at $\rho = 0.4$ (\S\ref{sec:tau-sensitivity}). All values in $[0.5, 0.8]$ improve over uniform training, with PPL differences spanning only 0.035 across this range (17.688 at $\tau{=}0.5$ to 17.653 at $\tau{=}0.8$). For $\tau > 0.8$, the solver inflates $b^*$ (mean $b^* = 2.36$ at $\tau{=}0.9$ vs.\ $1.40$ at $\tau{=}0.8$), causing excessive downweighting and degraded PPL ($17.712$ at $\tau{=}0.9$, $17.723$ at $\tau{=}0.95$). We adopt $\tau = 0.8$ as the default, noting the wide safe range $[0.5, 0.8]$ within which the method consistently outperforms uniform training (PPL $= 17.694$).

\paragraph{ESS floor $n_{\min}$.} The ESS constraint prevents gradient instability from extreme weight concentration. At $n_{\min} = 1$, the constraint is effectively removed: $b^*$ inflates to a mean of $7.20$ with 46.7\% of batches reaching $b^* \geq 2$, yielding PPL $= 17.789$---worse than uniform ($17.694$) by $+0.095$, confirming that unconstrained downweighting is harmful. At $n_{\min} = 2$, the method achieves consistent improvement across three random seeds (\S\ref{sec:main-results}). At $n_{\min} \geq B$ (batch size), the constraint forces $b^* = 0$ for all batches, degenerating to uniform training. We use $n_{\min} = 2$ as the default.

