\begin{equation}
    \frac{d\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}}{db} = \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(1 - \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}) \left( \mathbb{E}_w[\ln(1{-}q) \mid \mathrm{real}] - \mathbb{E}_w[\ln(1{-}q) \mid \mathrm{synth}] \right),
    \label{eq:dalpha}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbb{E}_w$ denotes expectation under the normalized weight distribution. Since real samples have lower $q$-scores, $\ln(1{-}q)$ is strictly less negative for real than synthetic samples, making the difference positive. For (2), increasing $b$ concentrates weight mass on fewer low-$q$ samples, strictly reducing ESS when weights are non-uniform. Full proofs are in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}.
